## 2.2. Preparation of FeMOFs
FeMOFs was prepared as the method described previously (Zhang et al., 2020). 2-aminoterephthatic acid $(0.126\ g)$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\left(0.187~\ g\right)$ , and acetic acid $(3.0~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (15 mL). The solution was subsequently reacted for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Next, the reaction mixture was treated by centrifugation and washed by N, N-dimethylformamide three times and ethanol once. At last, the collected solid was vacuum-dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of FeMOFs-sCuO- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot A b_{2}$
$100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathbf{Ab}_{2}$ 0 $\mathrm{[10\upmug\mathfrak{mL}^{-1})}$ , $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ EDC ( $\mathrm{\cdot}10\mathrm{mmol})$ and NHS (5 mmol) mixture PBS $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}$ solution and $1\mathrm{mL}$ PBS $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}$ were incubated at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Next, 1.5 mg FeMOFs-sCuO were added and incubated at $4^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then the mixture was centrifuged and dissolved in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS and then stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for further use.
## 2.4. Fabrication of the biosensor
The preparation procedure of the biosensor is relatively simple (Scheme 1). Glassy carbon electrode (GCE, $\varPhi=4\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) was given a 0.05 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ alumina slurry pretreatment to achieve a clean and clear surface. On the GCE’s surface, $6{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ -CoPdNPs were constructed and allowed to air dry. Then, 6 μL $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{b}_{1}\mathbf{was}$ attached, allowed by BSA $(3\upmu\mathrm{L},0.1\ \mathrm{wt}\%)$ to take up the nonspecific adsorption sites. Thereafter, a series of different concentrations of NSE $(6~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were spread onto the modified electrode, and stored for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $4~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ in a moist state. Finally, the ECL biosensor was manufactured after the immune recognition binding of 6 μL FeMOFs-sCuO- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{A}\mathbf{b}_{2}$ bioconjugates, followed by storing at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for later use. In the above construction process, the as-prepared electrode was softly rinsed with PBS buffer solution after each modification step to remove weakly-bound components.
## 2.5. ECL measurements
The classical three-electrode system was applied to investigate the ECL signal, in which $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ as reference electrode, platinum electrode as counter electrode, and the electrode with different modification states as working electrode. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was used to measure the electrochemical response between $-1.5$ and $0\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scanning rate of $0.15\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ . To achieve the best results and identify the ECL responses to NSE with a range of various concentrations, the measurement was per­ formed in ECL cell contained PBS $\mathrm{.10~mL}$ , pH 7.4) with $\mathrm{K_{2}S_{2}O_{8}}$ $\mathbf{\hat{\delta}}80\mathbf{\mM})$ . The voltage of photomultiplier tube was $680\mathrm{~V~}$ . Subsequently, the fabricated immunosensor was put in the cell and investigated the ECL performance. A digital photograph of the ECL experimental setup was shown in Fig. S1.
## 2.6. The procedure of analysis of NSE in serum samples
In order to conduct the recovery experiment, the human serum was obtained from a local hospital and diluted 10 times before use. First, the NSE concentration of the diluted serum was measured by using immu­ nosensor fabricated here (denoted as detected concentration). Then the samples were divided into four groups, and each group was spiked with 0.0500, 0.100, 0.500, $1.00~\mathrm{{ng~mL}^{-1}}$ of NSE standard samples, respec­ tively (denoted as addition). After measurement, the corresponding NSE concentrations of these samples were obtained.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was further applied to indicate the internal molecular structure of composite materials. The obvious diffraction peaks at $26.50^{\circ}$ and $46.19^{\circ}$ corresponds to the planes of (002) and (201) of $\mathrm{\bfP-C_{3}N_{4}}$ (Fig. 2A, curve a). When the CoPdNPs was combined, new peaks appeared in the overall diffraction pattern, which were at $33.98^{\circ}$ , $38.55^{\circ}$ , $40.53^{\circ}$ , $43.64^{\circ}$ and $51.54^{\circ}$ corresponding to the planes of (004), (301), (114), (220), (401) of Co elements, respectively. In addition, the peak values of $40.01^{\circ}$ , $46.53^{\circ}$ and $67.92^{\circ}$ also appeared in the diffraction pattern, corresponding to the planes of (111), (200) and (220) of Pd element, which confirmed the successful preparation of the $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ - CoPdNPs composite material (Fig. 2A, curve b). As shown in Fig. 2B, the XRD pattern of FeMOFs were consistent with those reported in the literature (Fig. 2B, curve a) (Ma et al., 2013). Specific peaks of sCuO were found at $35.56^{\circ}$ , $38.74^{\circ}$ , $48.74^{\circ}$ , $58.30^{\circ}$ , and $68.13^{\circ}$ (Fig. 2B, curve b), which corresponded to the planes of $(-111)$ , (111), ( 202), (202) and (220), respectively. It can also be seen that the characteristic peak of the diffraction peak is relatively sharp, indicating that the crystal structure of the product is relatively complete. In addition, the XRD pattern of FeMOFs-sCuO composites also contained obvious character­ istic peaks of FeMOFs and sCuO, which confirmed the successful syn­ thesis of the composites.
The fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was used to explore the functional groups of the synthesized materials (Fig. S3). In the FeMOFs spectrum, the amino group and $\mathtt{C}{=}0$ bond in the organic linkers of the FeMOFs were the cause of the strong peak at $3470~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1658~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively. The absorption peaks at $1390~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $685~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the C–O symmetric vibrations, and bending vibration inside and outside the plane of –COO group in FeMOFs, respectively. At $520~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , O–Fe–O bending vibrations in the FeMOFs were seen (Zangoa et al., 2020). As can be seen from the spectrum of sCuO, O–H stretching vibration of hydroxyl groups caused the infrared absorption peak at $3430~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ . These hydroxy groups can form hydrogen bonds between molecules. It was worth noting that absorption signals appear at $718~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $493~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were caused by the stretching vibration of Cu–O (Meher and Rao, 2013). The FeMOFs-sCuO spectrum contained the characteristic absorption peaks of both FeMOFs and sCuO, which confirmed the successful synthesis of FeMOFs-sCuO.
By obtaining the Barrett-Joy-ner-Halenda pore size distributions and nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms of these $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ samples, the textural characteristics of these materials were further studied (Fig. 2D). The specific surface areas of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ were found to be 248.52 and $\bar{3}39.04\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Additionally, the porosity within the nanoscale sheets of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ is reflected in its $2{\cdot}5\mathrm{nm}$ pore size dispersion. Compared with $\begin{array}{r}{g_{\checkmark}\mathrm{-C_{3}N_{4}},}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{\bfP-C_{3}N_{4}}$ has a higher distribution at $10{-}25~\mathrm{nm}$ , which was attributed to the formation of more holes after etched, while layerstacked pores are responsible for the distribution appeared at $30{\mathrm{-}}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
## 3.2. ECL mechanism
Herein, the enhancing and quenching strategies of ECL signal were illustrated by rigorous experiments. Fig. 3A showed the ECL strength of different luminescent material in PBS with $\mathrm{K_{2}S_{2}O_{8}}$ . The bulk $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ exhibited a low and unstable ECL signal, which was related to the block stacked structure of the original $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ . The prepared $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ exhibited an evident ECL emission intensity, because the specific surface area of $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ was increased by etching of $\mathrm{{siO}_{2},}$ and more ECL luminophores in the inner layers can be activated. After modification of CoPdNPs on $\mathrm{\bfP-C_{3}N_{4}},$ a higher and more stable ECL signal was realized. However, as shown in Fig. S4, the ECL responses of both $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}$ (line a) and $\mathrm{P-C_{3}N_{4}}.$ - CoPdNPs (line b) in PBS without $\mathrm{K_{2}S_{2}O_{8}}$ were weak. Based on the above
